  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday: Another warm and dry day is on tap for the Inland NW\nas a dry southwest flow continues. Highs will reach the mid \n60s to low 70s which is around 10 degrees above normal.\n\nThursday and Friday Night: Changes arrive as a low off the \nCalifornia coast ejects northeast into California, Nevada and \nsouthern Idaho. While the system is passing well to our south, \nsome of this moisture gets picked up by an incoming upper trough\nfrom the west, with precipitable water values increasing to \nnear 150 percent of normal across the eastern third of \nWashington and as much as 200 percent of normal in North Idaho. \nThis will translate to a 60-80 percent chance of rain over the \nCentral Panhandle Mountains down through the LC Valley, Camas \nPrairie, Blue Mountains, and Palouse and a 30-50 percent chance \nof rain from Ritzville up through Spokane, Newport, and the \nNorth ID Panhandle. Yet with the incoming trough from the west \nthis moisture increase is short lived which will likely keep \nprecipitation amounts to less than a tenth of an inch across \nEastern Washington with a tenth to quarter inch over the Central\nPanhandle Mountains and Camas Prairie. Drier air associated \nwith this trough will result in much drier air coming into \nCentral WA on Friday as precipitable water values plummet to \n25-50 percent of normal. This combined with breezy north-\nnorthwest winds (locally windy down the Okanogan Valley and \nWaterville Plateau) behind a cold front will promote elevated \nfire weather conditions and the potential for patchy blowing \ndust. Winds ease Friday Night, and with the very dry air over \nthe region radiational cooling will be strong, with freezing \ntemperatures for most (except locally around Wenatchee), with \nthe colder pockets such as Republic and Deer Park falling into \nthe low 20s.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday: Ensembles to continue to struggle with\na low that strengthens off the southern BC coast on Saturday and\nthen what happens after that. 40 percent of the clusters move\nthe low near or over the region for cooler and showery weather\nwhile the other 60 percent maintain ridging over the region.\nGiven the uncertainty not too many changes to this part of the\nforecast which currently provides a middle ground of the model\nsolutions with near seasonal temperatures with highs mainly in\nthe 50s and a 20-30 percent chance of showers. But with a 10-15\ndegree range between the NBM 10th and 90th percentiles there is\nlow confidence in the forecast details. If the cooler and\nshowery scenario pans out snow could fall as low as 3500 to 5000\nfeet in the Cascades and northern mountains but again confidence\nis low. JW",
